WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.890
SPEAKER: Welcome to
"Google Cloud Drawing

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.410
Board," where we doodle
our way through the cloud.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.500
Today's topic--
what is BigQuery?

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.900
BigQuery is Google Cloud's
enterprise data warehouse

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.480
designed to help you
ingest, store, analyze,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.700
and visualize big
data with ease.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.220
Organizations rely
on data warehouses

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.650
to aggregate data from
disparate sources,

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.890
process it, and make it readily
available for data analysis

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.540
that supports their
strategic decision-making.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.990
You can ingest data into
BigQuery either through batch

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.180
uploading or streaming
data directly

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.430
to deliver real-time insights.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.260
As a fully-managed
data warehouse,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.300
Google takes care of
the infrastructure

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.960
so you can focus on analyzing
your data up to petabyte scale.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.800
BigQuery supports the same
Structured Query Language,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.803
or SQL, for analyzing
your data, which

00:00:45.803 --> 00:00:47.220
you may be familiar
with if you've

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.500
worked with ANSI-compliant
relational databases

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:50.615
in the past.

00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:52.740
If you're looking to create
machine learning models

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.130
using your enterprise data,
you can do so with BigQuery ML.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.990
With only a few
lines of SQL, you

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.870
can train and execute
models on your BigQuery data

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.760
without needing
to move it around.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.730
When it comes time to
visualize your data,

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.750
BigQuery integrates
with Looker, as well as

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.610
several other business
intelligence tools

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.440
across our partner ecosystem.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.630
Now, how do you use BigQuery?

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.550
Luckily, it's
straightforward to get up

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.870
and running with BigQuery.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.720
After creating a GCP
project, you can immediately

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.870
start querying public data
sets, which Google Cloud hosts

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.360
and makes available
to all BigQuery users,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.810
or you can load your own data
into BigQuery to analyze.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.440
Interacting with BigQuery to
load data, run queries, or even

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.420
create ML models can be done
in three different ways.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.200
First is by using the UI
and the Cloud Console.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.930
Second is by using the
BigQuery command line tool.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.080
And third is by making
calls to the BigQuery API,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.170
using client libraries
available in several languages.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.690
BigQuery is integrated with
Google Cloud's Identity

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.420
and Access Management
Service so you can securely

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.730
share your data and
analytical insights

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.040
across the organization.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.960
What does it cost
to use BigQuery?

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.270
With BigQuery, you pay
for storing and querying

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.700
data and streaming inserts.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.160
Loading and exporting
data are free of charge.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.770
Storage costs are based on
the amount of data stored

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.949
and have two rates based on
how often the data is changing.

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:12.940
Query costs can be
either on demand,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.690
meaning you are charged per
query by the amount of data

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.900
processed, or flat rate for
customers who want to purchase

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.400
dedicated resources.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.450
Want to learn more
about BigQuery?

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.610
Check out
cloud.google.com/bigquery.

